<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>7.5.5: Practice</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01820</md:content-id>
    <md:title>7.5.5: Practice</md:title>
    <md:uuid>0a00d4b8-81af-4786-b132-5ba65285b38c</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section>
      <para>Complete the following questions to practice the skills you have learned in this lesson.</para>
      <section class="check-understanding">
        <exercise id="8b2410d3-bb2b-4626-b7a5-1bd9f96a758a" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>Here is a graph representing \(h\).</para>
            <para>A rocket is launched in the air and its height, in feet, is modeled by the function \(h\).</para>
            <para>
              <figure>
                <media alt="A curve on a graph, origin O, with grid. Horizontal axis, time, seconds, scale 0 to 8, by 1’s. Vertical axis, height, feet, scale 0 to 200, by 20’s. A curve passes through the points 0 comma 5, 3 comma 161, and 5 comma 105.">
                  <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_07_10_01.svg"/>
                </media>
              </figure>
            </para>
            <para><emphasis>Select three</emphasis> true statements about the situation.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! The rocket is launched from a height less than 20 feet above the ground. The rocket reaches its
        maximum height after about 3 seconds. The maximum height of the rocket is about 160 feet.
      </para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.5.2. </para>
            <para>The correct answers are: The rocket is launched from a height less than 20 feet above the ground. The rocket
        reaches its maximum height after about 3 seconds. The maximum height of the rocket is about 160 feet. </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="a7039bb0-885d-4513-8561-5888c03fbf04" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>Use this table to answer questions a–g.</para>
            <para>A baseball travels \(d\) meters \(t\) seconds after being dropped from the top of a building. The distance
          traveled by the baseball can be modeled by the equation \(d=5t^2\).</para>
            <table class="os-raise-skinnytable">
              <thead>
                <row>
                  <entry>
                    <emphasis>
              \(t\) (seconds)
            </emphasis>
                  </entry>
                  <entry>
                    <emphasis>
              \(d\) (meters)

            </emphasis>
                  </entry>
                </row>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <row>
                  <entry>
              0
            </entry>
                  <entry> </entry>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry>
              0.5
            </entry>
                  <entry> </entry>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry>
              1
            </entry>
                  <entry> </entry>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry>
              1.5
            </entry>
                  <entry> </entry>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <entry>
              2
            </entry>
                  <entry> </entry>
                </row>
              </tbody>
            </table>
            <para> How many meters \((d)\) did the baseball travel in 0 seconds \((t)\)? </para>
            <para> Enter your answer here:</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! 0. The baseball traveled 0 meters \((d)\) in 0 seconds \((t)\) because it has not been dropped yet.
      </para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.5.2 and 7.5.3.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is: 0. The baseball traveled 0 meters \((d)\) in 0 seconds \((t)\) because it has not been
        dropped yet. </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="d8614f9a-5498-4ad9-ad3b-f24715e8b6a3" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
            <para> How many meters \((d\)) did the baseball travel in 0.5 seconds (\(t\))?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! 1.25. The baseball traveled 1.25 meters (\(d\)) in 0.5 seconds (\(t\)).
      </para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.5.2 and 7.5.3.
      </para>
            <para>The correct answer is: 1.25. The baseball traveled 1.25 meters (\(d\)) in 0.5 seconds (\(t\)). </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="e8242ed2-1431-4717-91b8-338331d497fd" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
            <para> How many meters (\(d\)) did the baseball travel in 1 second (\(t\))? </para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para> Correct! 5. The baseball traveled 5 meters \((d)\) in 1 second \((t)\).
      </para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.5.2 and 7.5.3.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is: 5. The baseball traveled 5 meters \((d\\) in 1 second \((t)\).
      </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="385149f7-7acb-405a-b608-184a5306e8b7" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
            <para> How many meters (\(d\)) did the baseball travel in 1.5 seconds (\(t\))?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! 11.25. The baseball traveled 11.25 meters \((d)\) in 1.5 seconds \((t)\).</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.5.2 and 7.5.3.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is: 11.25. The baseball traveled 11.25 meters \((d\)\) in 1.5 seconds \((t)\).
      </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="29525aa0-e9f1-4f81-a7c4-de5b8d7d594d" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
            <para> How many meters (\(d\)) did the baseball travel in 2 seconds (\(t\))?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! 20. The baseball traveled 20 meters (\(d\)) in 2 seconds (\(t\)).</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.5.2 and 7.5.3.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is: 20. The baseball traveled 20 meters (\(d\)) in 2 seconds (\(t\)). </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="f86a8f64-56ea-4033-a2ec-5dc9a9dbad6f" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para> Is this graph an accurate representation of the data in the table? 
            
            <figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_07_10_02.jpg"/></media></figure></para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! Yes. The coordinates plotted on the graph match the data in the table: \((0,0)\), \((0.5,1.25)\),
        \((1,5)\), \((1.5,11.25)\), \((2,20)\).</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.5.2 and 7.5.3.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is: Yes. The coordinates plotted on the graph match the data in the table: \((0,0)\),
        \((0.5,1.25)\), \((1,5)\), \((1.5,11.25)\), \((2,20)\). </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="7cdcd3e2-4763-4178-9234-595a52e0abf9" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para> Is the baseball traveling at a constant speed?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! The distance traveled is not changing at a constant speed; it is increasing over time. For example, in
        the first half second, it travels 1.25 meters, and in the next half second, it travels 3.75 meters.</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.5.2 and 7.5.3.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is: No. The distance traveled is not changing at a constant speed; it is increasing over
        time. For example, in the first half second, it travels 1.25 meters, and in the next half second, it travels
        3.75 meters. </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="aa57ecbc-5dbb-43a0-aaa7-3ef042f52830" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>A small ball is dropped from a 100-foot-tall building. Which equation could represent the ball’s
          height, \(h(t)\), in feet, relative to the ground, as a function of time, \(t\), in seconds?</para>
            <para>Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para>\(h(t)=100-16t\)</para>
            <para>\(h(t)=100-16t^2\)</para>
            <para>\(h(t)=100+16t^2\)</para>
            <para>\(h(t)=100-\frac{16}t\)</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! \(h(t)=100-16t^2\)</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.5.4. </para>
            <para>The correct answer is \(h(t)=100-16t^2\).
      </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </section>
    </section>
  </content>
